I don't know about you but I've noticed  a very welcome uptick recently in the  number of fantasy books inspired by West  African folklore the afrofuturism and  African futurism movements have paved  the way for Black authors across the  diaspora to build worlds and tell  stories influenced by their Heritage  thanks to the hit success of books like  Tomi adeyemi's Children of Blood and  Bone wider audiences are finally getting  introduced to the orisha and spirits of  yarabalor but who are these deities and  where do they come from  foreign  's novel the main character is part of  the iku clan that worships the deity Oya  and traditionally has powers over life  and death Oya is a real figure from  yaraba mythology a collection of stories  from the widespread tribe that speaks  the yaraba language Oya is one of many  orisha or deities who can move between  the human and divine Realms with control  over the wind a reputation as a fierce  Warrior and a responsibility to watch  over the threshold of life and death Oya  is one of the more formidable yaraba  deities but she is by no means the most  powerful according to yaraba mythology  the universe and all of the forces  therein were created by a single Supreme  Being most often known as olefin also  known as alaron or olodamare this  genderless Creator God is all-knowing  all-powerful and eternal it might be  tempting to compare olefin to the  abrahamic God but they are a unique  being and no other deity is like them in  the beginning of time oliphan crafted  the universe and invented day and night  they gave Divine energy called ashay to  all the objects and beings and breathed  life or Emmy into every living thing  they did not however want to manage  every living thing so oliphan created  the orisha to act as intermediaries  between Arun the spiritual Heavenly  realm and Aya the world of tangible  visible things there are hundreds of  orisha and yaraba folklore though it may  be impossible to give an exact count  because their characteristics are  malleable a single orisha's name gender  and parentage can vary depending on when  and where you hear the story though  their core traits remain largely  unchanged some of the orisha like  albatala and Aran Mila are primordial  figures who were involved in the  creation of the world others like odudua  or Shango were mortal ancestors whose  Deeds LED them to be deified into the  religion and there were many orisha who  personified aspects of nature like the  spirits who embody the numerous rivers  and lakes in the world before the world  was created olefin and the Arisha lived  in the sky around a young Baobab Tree  below them stretched a vast ocean ruled  by the Arisha olukun most of the orisha  were content to explore the sky and  enjoy a life of luxury around their  Baobab tree but abattala wanted more he  looked at the vast ocean below and  thought hmm I could make something with  that so he asked oliphan if he could  build a resting place for living things  oliphan gave him permission to make land  but left it to abatala to figure out how  albatala went to oranmila the orisha of  Prophecy for guidance Oran Mila whose  power of divination made him the sole  witness to oliphan's creation of the  universe told albatala to Fashion a long  gold chain fill a snail shell with sand  and gather a chicken and a date palm nut  a batala traveled the sky asking the  other orisha for their spare gold which  he forged into a chain to carry him to  alakan's Ocean when abatala climbed to  the end of the chain he found that he  still had some distance to go from his  position in the sky oranmila told  obatala to pour sand from his shell onto  the ocean and immediately drop the  chicken when it landed the chicken began  to kick the sand around until it formed  a huge landmass with Hills and Valleys  abatala jumped down from his chain and  planted his date palm nut which  immediately sprouted into a full tree  that spot became IFA the legendary  cradle of yaraba civilization that  archaeologists believe was an urban  center by 8 800 CE abatala made some  wine from the fruit of his tree and  after partaking in perhaps too many  Libations began to mold people out of  clay  many of the figurines were imperfect  with missing limbs or unintended bends  but allyfin breathed Emmy into them  anyway when abatala saw his Creations  come to life he vowed never to drink  again and assumed responsibility for  protecting humans with physical  differences in some stories avatala got  drunk before he could complete his task  of making land and his younger brother  the Arisha odudua took over for him  regardless of who made the land  yarabalor tells of odudua or odua the  first king of Etha from whom the yaraba  people are descended alakin was Furious  that her ocean had been encroached upon  without her permission not even a heads  up from abatala so she sent Great Waves  to flood the land those lucky enough to  reach high hills besieged eschu the  messenger orisha of roads and doors to  intervene on their behalf Ashu told  oranmila about the flood and he used  abatala's golden chain to climb down and  cast spells until alican's water  retreated notice that the people begged  eschu to take care of the flood not the  Supreme Creator as the messenger orisha  eschu stands watch at the boundary  between the Realms oroon and Aya the two  halves of the yaraba cosmos eschu  fairies notes and sacrifices Across the  Threshold and looks over ritual  practices according to a yaraba saying  if eschu is not acknowledged during a  ritual then life is the bailing of  waters with a sieve in other words if  you're not going to honor eshu why even  bother this makes even more sense when  you consider that eschu is the trickster  of yaraba Mythology ritual sacrifice and  prayer are just two of the ways mortals  can connect to the orisha's wisdom and  power the other is through divination  that lets humans see the universal  forces that influence their lives so  oranmila is the other orisha who sits  between orun and Aya as olefin intended  now I don't know from person experience  but any parent can tell you that just  because you give a being life doesn't  mean they'll always follow your  directions the orisha are no different  and in one yaraba myth they decided they  were tired of listening to oliphan what  could a being who lives so far away from  the world possibly know about how to run  it they believed they could do a better  job maintaining the world without  oliphan's pesky influence instead of  punishing the orisha for their  disrespect olefin held the reins back  from the world rivers and lakes  evaporated away crops withered and  animals and humans alike died of thirst  they cried to the Arisha as they had  always done when distressed what have we  done to anger you the humans never  imagined that it was the distant Creator  God who was causing their pain the  orisha begged for oliphan's forgiveness  but their cries couldn't carry far  enough to be heard many of the orisha  tried to reach oliphant's Heavenly home  but they tired before they reached it  beautiful Ash the youngest of the orisha  who looks after the world's Sweet Waters  wanted to try the other orisha was  skeptical that someone so young could  accomplish such a difficult task but  Austin insisted she turned herself into  a beautiful peacock and flew towards  oliphan's distant home as she flew her  feathers began to fall off and as she  passed the fiery Sun her feathers  smoldered away she flew and flew and  flew until she didn't think she could  fly anymore but she finally reached  oliphan by the time she collapsed at  their feet ashen's feathers were all  gone instead of a beautiful peacock she  was an ugly vulture oliphan was touched  by Ocean's Sacrifice by her courage and  persistence and agreed to send the  Reigns back to the world for her  troubles oliphan told Ashen that she  alone as a vulture could communicate  directly with them after this tale  humans can commune with the orisha  through eshu but anyone who wants to  reach Olivet must go through Ashen given  her beauty and proximity to the Supreme  Creator it's no surprise that Ashen was  one of the wives of Shango the woman  loving orisha of thunder and lightning  together they produced ibeji a pair of  sacred twins to this day twins are  special to the yaraba and mistreating  them is said to bring about Misfortune  in history Shango was one of the first  yaraba Kings who ruled from the ancient  city Oyo and was closely related to  odudua in mythology Shango is one of  many who sprang from the body of the  orisha yamoja the orisha Shango briefly  occupied the body of the Mortal King but  also took other forms throughout his  Immortal Life the human King shango's  Reign was harsh and violent Legend says  that he was so relentless in his search  for power through magic that when he  finally obtained dominion over thunder  and lightning he accidentally used it to  destroy his palace with his wife and  children inside dismayed Shango fled his  kingdom and hung himself but his mighty  power was not forgotten Shango was  deified as an Arisha after his death or  should I say that his identity as the  orisha was discovered after the King's  death either way King shango's Spirit  didn't follow the typical path of other  dead Mortals Someone who lived a long  good life could expect to join the  ancestors in oroon after they died if it  was part of oliphan's plan they might be  reincarnated unlike hinduism's karmic  reincarnation where a soul can return in  any number number of random forms based  on their behavior in a previous life at  unwe is the belief that you will be  reborn into your own family your actions  in this life determine your family's  future Fortune so if you misbehave you  won't come back as a cockroach you'll be  born into poverty as your great-grandson  these stories and beliefs from yaraba  mythology are a small part of a colorful  Dynamic culture that goes back almost 2  000 years they are preserved in ishay a  yaraba philosophy way of life and  religion which is still practiced by  many today there are roughly 50 million  people spread between parts of Nigeria  Benin Ghana and Togo who speak the  yaraba language yaraba and ishay  influence can be seen in Caribbean  spiritual practices like Santeria and  condom ble but now that you know a bit  more about atunway the intervening  orisha and supreme olefin I'm willing to  bet that you'll start to notice when  West African influence all over modern  media but believe me when I say I've  barely scratched the surface of yaraba  Mythology here so comment below with  your favorite stories or figures from  yarabalur and don't forget to thank eshu  before you hit send  before you go if you're a fan of this  show there is a new series on PBS  Origins called Rogue history that we  know you'll enjoy there is a mythology  surrounding pirates that has infiltrated  our culture but how much of it is true  Rogue history examines pirate lore to  unravel historical myths on Earth lost  narratives and introduce you to  little-known characters it's the pirate  history you were never told check out  the link in the description below

